# Pull Request Body Contract

Four sections, in this order. A missing section is a blocked PR.

```markdown
## What changed

- One bullet per externally visible change, imperative mood.
- Name the invariant a change establishes, not the files it touched.

## Why

The property that did not hold before and holds now. State the constraint the
change was built under when one shaped the design.

## Validation

- Each command exactly as run, one per line.
- The result: counts, exit status, what the output showed.

## Residual risk

- Every gate not run, and why.
- Every accepted limitation of the approach.
- `no known gap` only when that is literally true.
```

## Rules

- Validation lists commands actually run in this branch. A command you intend
  to run belongs under Residual risk until it has run.
- An unavailable environment is a residual risk, not an omission. Name the
  missing configuration.
- This body is the permanent review record for the change. Rebase-merge keeps
  the individual commit messages, so the body is not the commit message. Write
  both to be read a year later by someone bisecting.
- When creating or editing PR descriptions, never use `gh pr edit` (on Ubuntu
  `gh 2.45.0`, it fails on the deprecated Projects-classic GraphQL schema and
  silently leaves text stale). Always update using GitHub REST PATCH
  (`gh api -X PATCH repos/<owner>/<repo>/pulls/<n> -F body=@<file>` or
  `scripts/edit-pr.sh`) and perform the verification
  readback (`gh api repos/<owner>/<repo>/pulls/<n> --jq '{title: .title, body: .body}'`
  or `scripts/check-pr-text.sh`) to detect and reject stale PR text.

## Worked example

`alphazede/bran-dev#14` states its gate output (`--fast`, 263 core and 38 CLI
tests) and discloses that the live external-host smoke test could not run
because the required environment configuration was unavailable, and that the
PR therefore stays draft. That disclosure is the pattern to copy.

