---
name: scan-software-supply-chain
description: >-
  Scan external software at acquisition, installation, import, update, or
  release admission. Use for newly downloaded or vendored binaries, installers,
  archives, packages, images, plugins, skills, MCP servers, agent tools,
  dependency changes, release artifacts, and explicit provenance requests. Do
  not use merely because an unchanged installed local tool is invoked.
---

# Scan Software Supply Chain

Treat externally acquired, dependency-delivered, or provenance-unknown software
as inert data until the scan reaches `PASS`. The control exists because
trusted-looking external tools and packages can silently upload source, execute
installers, or introduce compromised dependencies before ordinary code review
notices.

## Scope boundary

Do not invoke this skill for an already-installed, unchanged local tool merely
because it is being run or lacks a historical scan receipt. Apply the control
at acquisition or update; concrete evidence of tampering or unexpected
behaviour is a new scan trigger.

Invoke this skill when at least one condition applies:

- software bytes came from outside the current reviewed repository change,
  including a download, package cache, registry, attachment, vendored tree, or
  external generator;
- a dependency, lockfile, package, plugin, MCP server, agent/model tool,
  installer, bootstrapper, container image, or native binary is being added or
  changed;
- a locally built binary, archive, image, or installer is about to cross a
  release, deployment, or distribution boundary; or
- the owner explicitly asks for provenance, malware, dependency, upload, or
  supply-chain verification.

Do not invoke this skill solely because an agent or developer created or edited
ordinary repository source, tests, scripts, documentation, prompts,
configuration, or a policy skill. Treat model-authored code as locally authored
code: inspect the diff and use the repository's normal lint, tests, security
review, and gate requirements. A local script enters supply-chain scope only
when it downloads, installs, imports, vendors, or executes external software, or
when it becomes a release/distribution artifact.

Do not rescan an unchanged trusted local tool before each invocation. Rescan
only when its bytes, version, origin, wrapper/source policy, permissions, or
relevant scan rules changed, or when concrete evidence calls its trust into
question.

## Safety rules

- Do not execute the target, its installer, its package hooks, `--help`, or `--version` to discover what it is.
- Do not use `ldd` on an untrusted binary. Prefer `readelf`, `objdump`, and bounded `strings`.
- Do not upload proprietary artifacts, hashes tied to private releases, source, or secrets to public scanners.
- Do not install a scanner, ruleset, advisory database, runtime, or unpacking tool without owner approval. Scan any newly acquired scanner through this workflow before trusting it.
- Use existing local tools and databases first. Record unavailable or stale checks instead of silently weakening the result.
- Keep downloaded material and extraction outside the repository. List archive contents and reject traversal, absolute paths, device nodes, or unsafe links before extraction.
- Dynamic analysis is optional and comes only after static checks pass. Use a disposable, no-network sandbox with read-only inputs and an empty temporary home; obtain owner approval before any meaningful host, credential, network, or external-state interaction.
- Preserve existing files. Quarantine or delete only with explicit owner authorization.

## 1. Resolve the scan packet

Record:

- exact artifact paths and whether each is `downloaded`, `installed`,
  `imported`, `vendored`, or `release-artifact`;
- intended command or integration point;
- origin URL, repository, package registry, package name/version, installer, or producing command;
- expected digest, signature, release attestation, lockfile integrity, or package-manager ownership when available;
- scan boundary, exclusions, tool availability, and whether network access is required.

For an in-scope release artifact, include its manifest, vendored dependencies,
nested binaries, image layers, installer, and other distributed payloads. Do
not broaden the scope to ordinary source files merely because they produced the
artifact; those remain under normal code validation and review.

If origin, scope, or intended behavior cannot be established, continue static inspection but the maximum verdict is `INDETERMINATE`.

## 2. Inventory without execution

Use deterministic local commands first:

```bash
file -- <artifact>
stat -- <artifact>
sha256sum -- <artifact>
find -P <tree> -xdev -type f -print
find -P <tree> -xdev \( -type l -o -perm /6000 -o -perm /0022 \) -print
```

For a tree, hash regular files in a stable sorted order. Record symlink targets
separately and flag links escaping the scan root. Identify executable bits,
shebangs, setuid/setgid files, world-writable files, device nodes, sockets,
hidden payloads, nested archives, and file-extension/magic mismatches.

For archives, list before extraction with the appropriate existing tool, such as `tar -tvf` or `unzip -l`. Treat listing failures or unsupported formats as `INDETERMINATE`.

## 3. Verify provenance and integrity

Prefer primary sources and independent verification:

- compare the computed digest with an official HTTPS release manifest, signed checksum, transparency record, registry integrity field, or lockfile;
- verify signatures with an already-trusted system tool such as `gpgv`, `minisign`, or `cosign` only when the matching trusted key or identity policy is known;
- for OS packages, use package-manager ownership, repository policy, and verification commands;
- for language packages, reconcile the exact version and integrity/checksum against the lockfile and local package cache;
- for container images, use an already-local immutable digest or saved archive unless the owner approves a pull.

A matching hash proves identity, not safety. A missing or mismatched expected hash, signature, identity, version, or origin blocks use.

## 4. Run static security checks

Select the minimum relevant checks by artifact type and threat. Do not run every
available scanner by default. Record each selected command, tool version,
database/ruleset age when available, and exit status.

- Malware/signature scan: use an installed local ClamAV, YARA, or equivalent scanner when available.
- SBOM and vulnerabilities: use local `syft` inventory and `trivy`, `grype`, `osv-scanner`, or ecosystem auditors without silent database/rules downloads.
- Secrets: use `gitleaks` or `detect-secrets` in redacted mode when the external
  artifact contains source, configuration, archives, templates, or other
  credential-bearing material. Redact at the point of discovery: never let a
  found credential value reach a log, transcript, or report — the scan must not
  become a second copy of the secret it found.
  - **Extract before scanning packed formats.** Entropy detectors run on a packed
    binary (`.asar`, `.exe`, image layer, compiled bundle) match its integrity
    hashes and base64 blocks as `generic-api-key`/`private-key` — pure noise.
    Extract to a text tree first and scan that. A "secret" whose byte offset lands
    in an archive header or integrity block is a false positive; discard it.
  - **A named provider prefix outranks any entropy score.** A hit on a documented
    prefix in a *plaintext text/config file* is high-confidence regardless of
    entropy, and a plain high-entropy string in a binary is low-confidence
    regardless of length. Rank on this, do not flatten every rule hit into one
    list. Named prefixes include at least: `GOCSPX-` (Google OAuth client
    secret), `AKIA`/`ASIA` (AWS), `ghp_`/`gho_`/`ghs_` (GitHub), `sk-`/`sk-proj-`
    (OpenAI), `xoxb-`/`xoxp-` (Slack), `AIza` (Google API key), and any
    `-----BEGIN … PRIVATE KEY-----` block.
  - **Classify ownership — this is the disclosure trigger.** For each real
    credential, ask whose it is and where it was found. Your own secret in your
    own tree is an internal rotation. A **third party's live credential shipped
    inside a distributed artifact you acquired** is a vulnerability *in their
    product*: the correct action is coordinated private disclosure, not internal
    cleanup. Nearby vendor metadata (updater config, `owner`/`repo` fields,
    package manifest) identifies the owner.
- Source/static analysis: use repository-native lint and security tooling first, then local `semgrep`, `bandit`, `cargo audit`, `npm audit`, `pip-audit`, or equivalents as applicable. Do not use network-fetched rule packs implicitly.
- Native binaries: inspect headers, interpreter, imports, RPATH/RUNPATH, capabilities, hardening, notes, and bounded suspicious strings with `readelf`/`objdump`; do not load the binary.
- Scripts/installers: inspect all shell commands, downloads, self-update paths, persistence, privilege changes, credential reads, environment loading, telemetry, repository traversal, and outbound endpoints.
- Archives/images: scan both the container/archive and the safely extracted tree when extraction is justified.

Investigate unexpected upload, telemetry, remote configuration, self-update, credential access, shell download-and-execute, persistence, obfuscation, or repository-wide traversal as blocking until disproved.

## 5. Use dynamic analysis only when necessary

State the behavior that static analysis cannot resolve and the sandbox boundary that would answer it. Default boundary:

- no network;
- read-only artifact and source mounts;
- empty temporary home and environment allowlist;
- no host secrets, agent sockets, SSH/GPG material, Docker socket, browser profile, or cloud metadata;
- process, filesystem, and attempted-network logging;
- disposable teardown after evidence capture.

Do not call a sandboxed run `PASS` if the real product requires untested network, credentials, elevated privileges, or installer behavior.

## 6. Classify the result

Return exactly one verdict:

- `PASS`: provenance and integrity are consistent; required static checks completed; no malware, blocking vulnerability, secret, unsafe packaging, or unexplained behavior remains.
- `BLOCK`: malware; integrity/signature mismatch; critical or reachable high-severity issue; unexpected upload/credential access/persistence; unsafe installer/archive behavior; **a live credential shipped in the artifact**; or another demonstrated violation.
- `INDETERMINATE`: incomplete provenance, unsupported format, missing/stale required scanner data, unresolved behavior, or incomplete scope.

When a `BLOCK` is a **third party's** credential shipped in a distributed
artifact, the remediation is not internal rotation — it is coordinated private
disclosure to that vendor. Name it as such in the verdict and its action, so the
finding is surfaced as reportable rather than buried in the file list.

`BLOCK` and `INDETERMINATE` are fail-closed: do not execute, install, import,
commit, deploy, or otherwise adopt the artifact until it is remediated or the
owner explicitly accepts the named residual risk.

Report the scope, origins, hashes, commands and outcomes, findings, unavailable
checks, verdict, and residual risks. Store evidence in an existing authorized
packet or the session response; do not create standalone audit documents in a
repository unless its instructions authorize them.

Lead the report with a **`CREDENTIAL FINDINGS`** block whenever any secret rule
fired, even on a `PASS`. It is ranked by confidence and ownership — named-prefix
secret in plaintext config first, entropy-only binary hit last — and each entry
carries a recommended action (rotate internally, discard as false positive, or
prepare disclosure). A named-prefix hit never appears below the fold. Omission
is not an option: if the block is empty, say so explicitly, so a real credential
can never be lost inside an otherwise-passing file listing.

## Workflow integration

- Run this scan while acquiring or updating an in-scope artifact, before it is
  installed, imported, admitted to a dependency or release set, deployed, or
  distributed.
- Reuse a passing result when artifact digest, origin/source policy, scanner
  policy, and applicable scan rules still match. Repeat only affected checks
  after one of those fingerprint fields changes.
- For ordinary locally authored work, do not invoke this skill. Use diff review,
  repository-native lint and tests, risk-appropriate security review, and the
  required gate instead.
- This skill does not replace `$security-scan`, `$security-diff-scan`, threat modeling, tests, or the repository's current review workflow; use those workflows when their triggers apply.
