---
type: reference
title: Pull Request Workflow Authoring
okf_status: active
tags: [internal, operations]
---

# Workflow authoring

Use this guide for pull-request delivery profiles. Preserve an approved
local-only profile's manual hosted workflows; this checker deliberately rejects
manual-only workflows and is not its gate. Repository policy and existing
subscription authority still apply.

## Required CI rules

- Trigger on "'`pull_request` and `push` to `main` only. Never use
  `pull_request_target`. Leave workflow-level path filters unset so required
  checks are not left pending by a skipped workflow.
- Group concurrency by both `github.workflow` and `github.ref`, with
  `cancel-in-progress: true`.
- Detect changed paths once, then run each area only when its paths change.
  Keep the `hygiene` area unconditional. Give each area one unique, literal
  check name, `gate / <area>`; do not expand area jobs with matrices.
- Pin every action to a full 40-character commit SHA. Resolve the upstream
  revision and follow the software supply-chain scan procedure before adopting
  a new action or updating a pin; a SHA alone is not a safety assessment.
- Set workflow `permissions: {contents: read}`. Job overrides must be identical.
  Do not pass secrets, inherit secrets, bind environments, or expose credentials
  to fork PR code. Use checkout with `persist-credentials: false`.
- Use standard GitHub-hosted runners, never self-hosted runner labels or groups.

These implement the CI contract. See GitHub'"'s [workflow syntax](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/workflows-and-actions/workflow-syntax)
for event, permission, job and concurrency semantics.

## Adapt and check

Start with "'`../templates/ci.yml`. Replace the example `code` area with the
repository'"'s real areas and gate commands. Include their sources, tests,
dependency manifests, shared build configuration and workflow paths in the
inline filters. Add a matching "'`changes.outputs.<area>` and one dependent job
for every filter. Keep hygiene unconditional. `setup-pre-commit` owns the local
gate and hook setup; do not duplicate that procedure here.

The template reuses existing action pins. `dorny/paths-filter` runs with an
empty token and a full checkout to use git instead of the PR API, which would
need an additional permission. Its [documented git fallback](https://github.com/dorny/paths-filter#usage)
compares PRs with their base branch and main pushes with the preceding push.

Run `python3 <skill-dir>/scripts/check-workflows.py <workflow> [<workflow> ...]`
with installed PyYAML 6.x. Exit 0 means the declared structural rules pass;
exit 1 lists violations or unreadable/malformed YAML. No arguments exits 2.
The checker supports this explicit `changes`/inline-filter/area-job shape;
unsupported expressions, reusable workflows, local actions and matrices fail
closed. It does not execute workflows or inspect referenced actions or gate
scripts. Inspect actual area coverage and script behavior, and observe a real
PR and push run before claiming operational proof. Pinning does not establish
that an action is safe or that shell code cannot leak data.

## Hosted budget

Standard hosted runners are free on public repositories. The
`gh_private_minutes` budget is 2,000 included minutes per account per month on
GitHub Free for private repositories, shared across those repositories; account
plans and runner multipliers affect consumption. Verify current entitlement
and remaining usage in [GitHub Actions billing](https://docs.github.com/en/billing/concepts/product-billing/github-actions).
Keep paid overage at zero with a hard stop. On exhaustion, stop hosted runs,
retain local gate evidence and report hosted evidence unavailable. Never enable
paid overage without owner authority or label missing hosted evidence PASS.
